---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="How Foxhole Forecast settles and scores timed model predictions." />
    <title>Scoring Method · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">EVENT OUTCOME PROTOCOL V5 · CONTINUOUS RANKED PROBABILITY SCORE</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
          <h1>How forecasts<br /><span>earn credit</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">A prediction names the base, outcome, ETA, event probability, and timing uncertainty. The official War API supplies the result.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to live scores</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Scoring sections">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href="#forecast">Forecast</a>
        <a href="#settlement">Settlement</a>
        <a href="#timing">Timing</a>
        <a href="#outcome">Outcome</a>
        <a href="#aggregation">Leaderboard</a>
        <a href="#censoring">Censoring</a>
        <a href="#rationale">Rationale</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="scoring-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The short version</p>
        <p>Each bet defines a probability distribution over when its named event will occur. CRPS compares that cumulative forecast with the observed API transition interval at every minute through ETA + 3 hours. Lower is better; 0 minutes is perfect.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Headline score</strong><span>A balanced 0–100 forecast score made from separate short- and long-bet CRPS. Higher is better; raw CRPS minutes remain visible.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section id="forecast" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01</p><h2>What a model must forecast</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid">
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>8</strong><h3>Unique bases</h3><p>Every round contains exactly eight ranked predictions on eight different strategic bases.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>4 + 4</strong><h3>Two ETA priorities</h3><p>Ranks 1–4 are immediate priorities and ranks 5–8 are extended priorities. The model gives exact minutes; an ETA near the six-hour boundary is retained and scored on the time it named.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>2</strong><h3>Allowed events</h3><p><b>Captured</b> means ownership completes a change to the opposing faction. <b>Destroyed</b> means a faction-owned base becomes neutral—demolished and not yet rebuilt.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>1–5</strong><h3>Evidence references</h3><p>Each call cites cutoff-safe metrics and rates their relevance from 1–10. Evidence ratings are shown for auditability, but are not judged or added to the score.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">Each prediction includes confidence from 0.00 to 1.00—the probability that the named outcome happens by ETA + 3 hours—and <code>sigma_minutes</code> from 15 to 180—the standard deviation of event time conditional on it happening. A same-faction capture call is dropped as a model error. Rank records priority but does not change weight.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="settlement" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">02</p><h2>How a prediction resolves</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Finite deadline</h3><p>Each prediction remains open through <code>ETA + 3 hours</code>. We look for the first physical ownership transition for that base after the round cutoff and before that deadline.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Destruction before capture</h3><p>A faction-owned base normally becomes neutral before an enemy rebuild completes. If the model called a faction-specific capture, we wait through the deadline for that follow-up. The named enemy capture earns full outcome credit; destruction without a completed capture earns 0.75. Invalid same-faction capture calls are dropped and explained on the round page.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">If no transition occurs by the deadline and coverage is adequate, the observed cumulative event curve stays at zero. CRPS then penalizes the probability the model assigned to an event that did not happen; no artificial infinite ETA is needed.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="timing" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">03</p><h2>The event-time distribution</h2></div>
        <p>The conditional event time is modeled as a Normal distribution centered on the model’s ETA with standard deviation <code>sigma_minutes</code>. It is truncated to the scoring window. Confidence supplies its total probability mass; the remaining mass means no qualifying event by the deadline.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">
          <span>forecast curve</span>
          <strong>F(t) = confidence × truncated Normal CDF(t; ETA, sigma)</strong>
          <span>CRPS</span>
          <strong>integral from cutoff to ETA + 3h of (F(t) − observed cumulative event(t))² dt</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="method-caption">The API gives an interval rather than an exact event second. We average the observed event time uniformly across that interval, so the score respects the collection cadence without pretending to know more than was observed.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="outcome" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">04</p><h2>Outcome handling</h2></div>
        <div class="outcome-table" role="table" aria-label="Outcome credit table">
          <div class="outcome-table__row outcome-table__head" role="row"><span>Prediction vs. observation</span><span>Outcome credit</span></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Captured by named faction / Named faction capture</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Destroyed / Destroyed</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Faction capture / Destroyed, or Destroyed / Faction capture</span><strong class="score-value">0.75</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>No qualifying transition by the deadline</span><strong class="score-value score-value--miss">0.00</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block formula-block--compact"><span>observed curve height</span><strong>1.00 for the named outcome · 0.75 for capture/destruction confusion · 0.00 for no qualifying outcome</strong></div>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Worked example</p>
          <h3>Correct capture, but later than expected</h3>
          <p>The forecast CDF rises near the ETA while the observed curve rises one hour later. The squared area between those curves is the bet’s CRPS in minutes.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="aggregation" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">05</p><h2>Leaderboard and historical sigma</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Two raw CRPS results</h3><p>Ranks 1–4 produce the short CRPS and ranks 5–8 produce the long CRPS. Each is the arithmetic mean in minutes across scored bets in that timeline. Lower is better; open and censored bets are excluded.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Legacy forecasts</h3><p>Older bets did not contain sigma. Their timing uncertainty is deterministically inferred as <code>max(15, 180 × (1 − confidence))</code> minutes and labeled as inferred. Their original confidence is preserved.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block">
          <span>fixed timeline scales</span>
          <strong>short = 540 minutes (6h + 3h) · long = 1,620 minutes (24h + 3h)</strong>
          <span>balanced forecast score</span>
          <strong>100 × [1 − ½(short CRPS ÷ 540) − ½(long CRPS ÷ 1,620)]</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="method-caption">The constants come from the protocol, not a model’s chosen ETA. Short and long performance therefore contribute exactly 50% each even while long bets are still resolving. A model needs at least one scored bet in both timelines before receiving a topline score. The result is bounded from 0 to 100 for display and is not a percent-accuracy claim.</p>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Why retain confidence too?</p>
          <p>Confidence answers “does this named outcome happen by the deadline?” Sigma answers “if it happens, how tightly can I locate its time?” CRPS evaluates both in one forecast distribution.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="censoring" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">06</p><h2>When we decline to score</h2></div>
        <p>A prediction is <b>censored</b> when the observations cannot support a fair result. Censored is a completed settlement state, but it contributes neither credit nor a miss to the model average.</p>
        <ul class="rationale-list">
          <li><strong>Missing coverage.</strong> A gap greater than two expected polling intervals—currently 30 minutes—means we may have missed or badly localized a transition.</li>
          <li><strong>Ambiguous transition interval.</strong> Normal polling intervals are integrated directly. A gap larger than the coverage tolerance is censored because it no longer localizes the event fairly.</li>
          <li><strong>War boundary.</strong> If a war ends before a prediction’s ETA-plus-three-hour scoring window closes, and no qualifying transition was already observed, the prediction is censored rather than treated as a miss.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="rationale" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">07</p><h2>Why score it this way?</h2></div>
        <ol class="rationale-list rationale-list--numbered">
          <li><strong>Make every claim falsifiable.</strong> A named base, event, exact ETA, and confidence is more informative than “something may happen in 24 hours.”</li>
          <li><strong>Force and balance short- and longer-range judgment.</strong> The four-within-6h plus four-from-6–24h split prevents a model from filling a round only with safer, vague next-day bets. Fixed timeline scales keep longer CRPS integration windows from dominating the topline score.</li>
          <li><strong>Match scoring precision to observation precision.</strong> Interval-aware CRPS uses the whole API observation interval rather than inventing an exact event minute.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognize the structure of Foxhole captures.</strong> Destroyed and captured are distinct outcomes, but both correctly anticipate loss of the current owner; the 0.75 rule captures that shared information.</li>
          <li><strong>Close every fair bet.</strong> The three-hour grace window ensures non-events receive a finite probabilistic penalty rather than remaining unresolved indefinitely.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid subjective grading.</strong> Evidence and relevance ratings remain visible for human review, while numerical settlement depends only on prospective forecasts and official API observations.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <p class="method-version">Current protocol: <code>event_outcome_v5_crps</code>. Existing event-outcome rounds are rescored with inferred sigma; new rounds ask the model for sigma directly. A new war receives a two-hour telemetry warm-up before forecasting begins.</p>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
